// SPDX-License-Identifier: LGPL-3.0-or-later
//
// A NULLABLE element source meets @PLAN52's bracket rule, exactly as its dense twin does.
//
// The rule is a blanket requirement on the SPELLING: `vector += elem` is refused whatever the
// element's type, because `vector<vector<T>> += vector<T>` is both "push one element" and
// "concatenate" and the brackets are what separate them. Asked with an unpeeled source it was
// not recognised on a `τ?`, so `d.c += n` with `n: integer?` was ACCEPTED where the dense
// `d.c += 9` is refused — the `?` spelling of one statement more permissive than the plain one,
// which is backwards for a nullability marker (loft#1223).
//
// Not a design call, and the precedent is one paragraph up in the same function: @PLN25 met this
// axis once already on the DESTINATION and answered *match on STORAGE, nullability creates no
// exception* — a `vector<T>?` is refused the same way and gets the same cure. This is the SOURCE
// side of that reading. The code changes to match the rule, not the other way round.
//
// The two questions stay separate and both still reach the reader: this refusal says WRITE THE
// BRACKETS, and `(N-Store)` says the value may be null where a non-null is expected. They are
// different questions about the same statement and neither substitutes for the other.
//
// ⚠ The cure this names is currently UNDER-DIAGNOSED, and that is loft#1232 rather than a reason
// to keep the acceptance: `d.c += [n]` stores the null into a dense `vector<integer>` with no
// diagnostic at all, on both backends and on the shipped 2026.8.0 — the whole vector-literal
// family is silent, including `v: vector<integer> = [n]` and a struct constructor. So closing
// this issue moves that reader from warned to silent until loft#1232 lands. That is the right
// trade — a rule violation should not ship to preserve a warning the CORRECT spelling ought to
// carry too — but it is a real gap and it is named here so the next reader does not rediscover
// it from our own diagnostic.
//
// The positive half is `1223b-a-bracketed-nullable-element-still-appends.loft`, in its own file
// because a firing `@EXPECT_ERROR` stops a file before it runs.
//
// @falsified-at: b82d37d3 — interpret exit 1 -> 0, native exit 1 -> 0
//   The direction inverts for an `@EXPECT_ERROR` file: the corpus runner scores it by whether the
//   expectation MATCHES, so the parent — where nothing fires — is the run that exits 1. Both
//   cells COMPILE and run there, so a naive exit reading of the PROGRAM would call them clean:
//   each appends its element and answers a correct length, with only an `(N-Store)` warning.
//   ⚠ That control is on no public ref, so this receipt records a falsification that
//   cannot be re-run — see TESTING.md § The patch receipt for the form that can be.

struct D1223 { c: vector<integer> }

// The filed shape: a nullable element with a VALUE in it.
// @EXPECT_ERROR: vector `+= elem` is ambiguous
fn test_1223_a_nullable_element_needs_brackets() {
  d1223a = D1223 { c: [11] };
  n1223a: integer? = 9;
  d1223a.c += n1223a;
  assert(len(d1223a.c) == 2, "unreached");
}

// An ACTUALLY-null source, because "the value happens to be present" is not what the rule reads —
// it reads the TYPE, and a cell where the runtime value is absent says the refusal is not
// accidentally value-dependent.
// @EXPECT_ERROR: vector `+= elem` is ambiguous
fn test_1223_an_absent_nullable_element_needs_brackets_too() {
  d1223b = D1223 { c: [11] };
  z1223b: integer? = null;
  d1223b.c += z1223b;
  assert(len(d1223b.c) == 2, "unreached");
}
